PATCH 556: four of board:0015's five remaining dispatch targets.

`FUN_0000_c4df` picks each object's render method from a per-VIEW table in STRSEG, and ten of the 24
distinct targets were never promoted by Ghidra -- the dispatch resolved to nothing.  Patches 533/534/535
landed five of them.  This lands four more; the fifth (`c783`) is NOT here and the reason is below.

    c715   view 0x1e, 1 slot
    c74d   view 0x20, 1 slot
    c774   view 0x1e, 1 slot
    c7a5   view 0x20, 1 slot

Each is a verbatim transcription of its asm, quoted in full at the site, using the conventions patches
310/463/535 established: SI from `g_fist_render_si`, the record cursor threaded through
`g_fist_render_di`, and the carry protocol through `g_fist_cf` (CF=1 "nothing drawn, keep walking";
`c8b2` and `c8c2` both end in `clc`).

## What made these four writable

board:0015 recorded these as blocked on "the c8c2 argument convention, or the STRSEG deferred-object
list write".  Both are settled by reading the callees:

- **`c8c2` takes only AH.**  Its asm forces `AL=0x10` itself (`c8cc: b0 10`), and the port's patch-310
  body renders that as `CONCAT11((char)(param_1 >> 8), 0x10)`.  So `c774`'s inherited AL is a
  DON'T CARE, and `c715` -- which does `mov %ax,%bx ; xor %ah,%ah` -- is fully determined.
- **The STRSEG append is unambiguous in the asm.**  `c74d` and `c715`'s else-arm share it verbatim:

      c75b: 8b 1e e0 e6        mov   0xe6e0,%bx          ; the deferred-object count
      c75f: 83 fb 40           cmp   $0x40,%bx
      c762: 73 0e              jae   0xc772              ; bound: 0x20 entries
      c764: 8e 2e 70 00        mov   0x70,%gs            ; GS = STRSEG, as 5fb0 and a5dc already do
      c768: 65 89 b7 6a 36     mov   %si,%gs:0x366a(%bx)
      c76d: 83 06 e0 e6 02     addw  $0x2,0xe6e0

  The port already writes STRSEG this way in `FUN_0000_5fb0` (patch 526) and `FUN_1000_a5dc`, so there
  was nothing new to establish -- only to read.

## Why `c783` is still not here

`c783` sets AL and inherits AH:

    c79d: b0 0e    mov  $0xe,%al
    c79f: e8 10 01 call 0xc8b2

`c8b2` forces AL=0x12 (so c783's own `mov $0xe,%al` is DEAD in the original) and passes AX through to
`ca2f`, which stores the WHOLE word: `ca3a: ab  stos %ax,%es:(%di)`.  So AH reaches the record and
matters -- and neither `c4df` (which sets DX, not AX: `c4fd mov 0xe48c(%bx),%dl ; c501 xor %dh,%dh`)
nor its view-0x22 arm at `c559` sets AH.  It is threaded from further up.  Writing it would mean
inventing a value that lands in a live record field, so it is left for whoever establishes AH's source.

